sections.text:
Arena is an Open Foris (OF) cloud platform for forest inventory data collection, management, analysis, dissemination, and reporting. It combines two current tools, namely OF Collect and OF Calc, into a seamless application. Open Foris Arena is the main entry point for data collected in field-based inventories. It provides a fast, easy, flexible way to set up a survey with a user-friendly interface. Arena manages multiple data types and complex validation rules, all in a multi-language environment.
Open Foris Arena provides an online solution for field data management, allowing full customization of inventory structure, variables and data checks. Arena promotes data quality through an integrated data entry and data cleansing workflow. Arena contains a formal description (i.e., metadata) of the types of variables, classifications and coding schemes used by the inventory. Older OF Collect surveys and data can also be imported into Arena.
What are the main advantages of Arena over the older Open Foris tools?
- No need for user installations;
- All data stored in a secure server in the cloud (hosted by Amazon Web Services, AWS);
- Data sharing and collaboration possible in a working group;
- Easier to maintain: all updates available at the same time for all users;
- No need for trained personnel to maintain the hardware and database management system.
What does OF Arena offer?
- Dynamic form designer;
- Multilingual data entry forms;
- Editor for entering complex validation rules;
- Categories and taxonomies management;
- Multiple surveys management;
- Survey templates management;
- Multi-cycle survey management;
- Map with access to satellite data;
- Map queries using the power of Google Earth Engine via Earth Map;
- Sample-based image interpretation;
- Import for OF Collect survey and data;
- Possibility to use multiple species lists; a species list can contain extra properties;
- Concurrent data entry with workflow and real time updates;
- User management with personal access rights;
- Survey backup (export, import), and cloning;
- Dashboard with activity logs;
- Light and dark theme, and user interface in several languages;
- Two-factor authentication (2FA) for every user;
- Data query, cleansing, export;
- Local off-line installation and usage via docker;
- Data analysis with local RStudio or RStudio Server.
The user support is available via Open Foris Support Forum at https://www.openforis.support/
Tag your question as follows:

OF Arena can host any type of inventory or questionnaire data. A typical process when working with Arena will be as follows:
- Log in to the platform;
- Select a survey;
- Depending on the user role: view, input, validate, edit, or export data;
- Data analysis with the help of R scripts [roles: Survey Administrator, Data Analyst],
- View and export the results and reports;
- Log out from the platform.

To open your user profile, click on your profile picture in the top-right corner and select My profile.
The page shows a summary of your account at the top (picture, name, email and roles) and groups the settings in sections; click a section name on the left to scroll to it:
- Change picture: drop an image or click Upload, adjust it with Scale and Rotate, then click Close;
- Personal information: your Title and Name (your email address is your login and cannot be changed here), the Institution and Country given when registering (shown only, not editable) and the Extra properties assigned to you (see 6.1.4);
- Sign-in & security: your password (Change password, see Chapter 13), the providers linked to your account (see 3.5) and your 2FA devices (Manage 2FA, see 3.4);
- Integrations: your own API key for Planet basemaps in Map API Keys (click Test to check it);
- AI features (only when AI features are enabled in Arena): enable the AI assistance and choose the AI provider; the section is not shown when AI features are disabled (see 3.6);
- Notification preferences (system administrators only): receive an email for New user access requests, or for New user self-registrations when users create their account without requesting access (off by default).
When you change something, a bar at the bottom of the page shows You have unsaved changes: click Save to save all your changes.
The Access & permissions section shows whether you are a System administrator or a Survey manager; these permissions are changed by the system administrators (see 11.4).

When AI features are enabled in Arena by the system administrators, you can get AI assistance in several parts of Arena. Each user decides what AI can see and which provider it uses: open My profile and go to the AI features section.
In What AI can see, choose the content that may be sent to the AI provider:
- Off: no AI feature is used;
- Help only: your question and passages from the Arena user manual;
- + Survey design: also entities, attributes, labels, categories and expressions, but no record values;
- + Record data: also record values and query results (by default, only an AI server on your device may receive them).
Click Customize to turn single features on or off; What it sends (the icon next to each feature) describes the content sent by the feature. Click Save to save your choice.
In Providers, Arena default is the provider configured by the system administrators (if any). Click Add provider to use your own: a Local server on your computer or network (e.g. LM Studio or Ollama, the content does not leave your network), an Organisation server or a cloud provider with your API key (e.g. OpenAI, Anthropic or Google Gemini). Click Test to check the connection, then add the provider; use the menu of a provider to Make default, Test connection, edit or remove it.
The AI assistant button in the header opens the assistant panel. Its tabs are the areas it works on, depending on the screen you are using and on what AI can see:
- Design: creates and changes entities, attributes, categories and labels in the Survey Designer;
- Analysis: answers questions about the data by running queries (Data Explorer and dashboard);
- Cleaning: looks for errors in the records and proposes fixes;
- Help: answers questions about Arena with passages from this manual.
The changes proposed by the assistant are applied only after you review and accept them. The Suggestions list shows tasks the assistant can do for you (e.g. labels missing in a language): click a suggestion, then Do it. Click New conversation to start a new one; previous conversations are kept in your browser.
Other AI features are available in the related screens:
- Expressions: in the expression editor, Generate expression from description writes an expression from a description in plain language, and Explain this expression explains an existing expression;
- Label translation: Translate to other languages in the labels editor and AI: Translate labels in the advanced menu of the Survey Designer;
- Data dictionary: Schema summary + AI descriptions (CSV or Excel) in the advanced menu of the Survey Designer;
- Activity summaries: Summarize in the activity log of the dashboard;
- Data queries: Ask AI in the Data Explorer generates a query from a description of the data you are looking for (see 9.6).
The AI policy of a survey (see 6.1.7) can limit where its content may be processed: a provider not allowed by the policy cannot be used in that survey. Always review the results generated by AI before saving them.

The AI policy tab (shown when AI features are enabled in Arena) lets the survey administrators choose where AI may process the content of the survey, separately for the Survey design (schema) (node definitions, labels and expressions) and the Record data (collected values and user activity):
- Off: AI is never used for this content;
- On this device: processed only by an AI engine running on the user's device (users need a local AI server, e.g. LM Studio);
- Organisation server: processed by an AI server run by your organisation;
- Cloud: may be processed by a cloud AI provider.
The record data cannot be more permissive than the survey design. The stricter of this policy and the limits of the Arena server applies to every user of the survey. Click Save to save the policy; only survey administrators can change it.

The Preview button at the top right of the Form Designer lets you test the forms while you design them, without creating real records. The main part of the button opens the desktop preview; the arrow next to it opens a menu with the preview options:
- Preview (desktop): opens a test record in the data entry form of Arena. The data entered in the preview is not saved among the survey records. The form is shown in an orange frame with a Preview Mode label at the top: select × in the label to close the preview.
- Preview (mobile): opens the Mobile preview panel on the right side of the page, with an approximation of Arena Mobile running in the browser with the draft survey. It is available to all survey designers, when the server provides the Arena Mobile preview.
In the toolbar of the mobile preview you can choose Phone or Tablet, Portrait or Landscape orientation, Reload the preview after changing the survey, and open the info button that lists the differences from Arena Mobile on a real device:
- dates and times are typed in a text field instead of the device date/time pickers;
- maps are not available;
- the location is taken from the browser (usually less precise than the device GPS);
- photos and files are selected from the computer (no camera capture and no photo location);
- the data is kept in memory only and lost when reloading; there is no records list, sync or export.
The preview can also be made available to users who cannot open the Form Designer (e.g. Data Editors), for example for user acceptance testing of a new version of the form before publishing it: check Allow data entry users to preview the draft (not published) form in the Security tab of the Survey Info and publish the survey. These users will then see a Preview form button in the Records list (with the same desktop and mobile options): it opens the form with the latest unpublished changes, and the data entered in it is not saved. Survey editors (e.g. Survey Managers and Survey Administrators) always see the Preview form button in the Records list, whether this option is checked or not.

To update a species list, click Import in the taxonomy editor and select the updated CSV/Excel file. If the survey is not published yet, the taxa of the file replace the existing ones. If the taxonomy is published:
- the taxa with the same code are updated and the new taxa are added;
- the taxa are sorted as in the imported file;
- published taxa that are not in the file cannot be deleted: they are left unchanged, after the imported ones, and a message lists their codes.
When importing Excel (.xlsx) files (taxonomies and categories), cells containing formulas, rich text or hyperlinks are read as plain values (the result of the formula, the text of the cell).

In the case of a multi-cycle survey, a Data Analyst (or a user with a higher role) can clone records from one cycle to another. The following conditions apply:
- The survey should contain more than 1 cycle;
- The survey must be published;
- Only data from older cycle can be cloned to newer, i.e. from Cycle 1 to 2, or from Cycle 2 to 3, but not backwards;
- Records cannot be overwritten in the target cycle; already existing records are skipped;
- Record key attribute definitions, defined on the Form Designer, must be the same in source and target cycle; if they have been modified, cloning will not work;
- The Clone-button is not shown on the last cycle’s record list;
When you want to clone data, do as follows:
- Select the source cycle in the cycle selector at the top of the page, e.g.
- Select the records to be cloned If you want to clone all records, you do not need to make any selection.
- Click on
- Select the target cycle (To cycle); only later cycles can be selected,
- Select whether you want to clone all records or only the selected ones:
- Click on Start cloning and confirm. When cloning is complete, a message shows how many records were cloned.

You can add layers published by map servers, for example national forest cover maps or recent satellite mosaics. Open the Add data menu and choose Add a WMS / WMTS service…:
- Service URL: the GetCapabilities URL of a WMS or WMTS service, or an XYZ tile template containing {z}/{x}/{y} (e.g., https://tiles.example.org/forest-cover/{z}/{x}/{y}.png). Click Load layers: for a WMS / WMTS service, select the layers to add in the list (only layers available in Web Mercator can be added); an XYZ template needs no capabilities;
- Add as: Overlay layer (shown over the base map, listed in the Layers tab, group Custom (WMS / WMTS)) or Base map (replaces the base map, listed in the Base map menu);
- Display name, Image format (PNG transparent or JPEG, when the service offers both), Opacity and Attribution;
- Who sees it: Everyone in this survey (the layer is saved in the survey and shown on the map of all its users; available to users who can edit the survey) or Only me (saved for you only).
Click Add 1 overlay (or Add 1 base map). Unlike uploaded files, these layers are kept when you reopen the map. To remove an overlay, click the × button next to it in the Layers tab; to remove a custom base map, select it and use Remove selected custom base map in the Base map menu. Only users who can edit the survey can remove the layers shared with everyone: for other users the layer is hidden until the map is reopened.
Note: services that use http (not https) may be blocked by the browser.

Click the Screenshot button (camera icon) in the top bar to save an image of the map. The Map screenshot dialog shows a preview and the following options:
- Capture: Current view or Fit to plot (the current view when the map is zoomed in and the selected plot is well inside it, otherwise the map centred on the plot, zoomed in when needed);
- Include: Markers and measurements, Scale bar and north arrow, and a stamp with Plot, date, coordinates, base map;
- Save to: Attach to a plot record (the image is saved in an image attribute of the plot: choose the Plot record, the Image attribute and the File name) or Download only (PNG file on this computer).
Click Save (or Also download to save it in the record and download it too). Images saved in a record are available to the field teams in Arena Mobile once they download the record. The Plot record list shows only the plots visible on the map when the dialog is opened, nearest to the centre first. Only the image attributes of the plot that you can edit and that have the Allow saving screen capture in map view property checked in the form designer are listed. Base map tiles whose server does not allow it are left out of the screenshot.

Always before using data import in Arena, take a backup of your survey with data. Read more here.
Arena can import external data from
- CSV or Excel files;
- Open Foris Collect / Collect Mobile files;
- Arena (backup) / Arena Mobile (export) files;
A survey should be published before a data import is possible.
If you are adding new information into the database using CSV or Excel files, import your data by following a top-down method! In the case of the survey structure described in the next chapter, first enter CLUSTER data as new records, then PLOT data with "update existing records", and then similarly "Reference objects", and so on. Data import template files are numbered to make data importing a bit easier.

The ‘Clone chain from another survey’ feature lets a Survey Administrator or Data Analyst copy a complete chain from a survey they have access to into the survey they are currently working on. The clone operation copies:
- the chain's properties (name, labels, and description);
- all R scripts;
- the calculated result variables (analysis attributes) defined in the chain, re-created under the matching entities of the destination survey;
- the categories and taxonomies referenced by those calculated variables, so that coded results keep working after the clone.
The cloned chain is created as a new, independent chain in the destination survey - the source chain is left untouched, and the two chains are not kept in sync after cloning.

To edit a user of the current survey, open Users > Users list and click the user. The page shows a summary of the user at the top (name, email, role and invitation status) and the following sections:
- Personal information: title, name, email and extra properties of the user;
- Access & permissions: the Role in current survey and, for system administrators, the System administrator and Survey manager permissions and the Max surveys user can create;
- Sign-in & security (system administrators only): the sign-in methods of the user, with Change password and Unlink;
- Remove from survey: revokes the access of the user to the survey, after a confirmation.
Click Save in the bar at the bottom of the page to save the changes.
When the user has not accepted the invitation in time, the page shows that the invitation expired: click Send new invitation to send it again.

Users with the System administrator privilege see the System Administration menu at the bottom of the sidebar. It is not linked to the current survey and contains the following items:
- Users access requests: requests sent with the Request access link of the login page, or accounts created with the Create an account link, shown with the status Self-registered (shown only when access requests are enabled on the server);
- Users list (all): all the users of the server, with Export to Excel, Backup / Restore and New User;
- Job monitor: the background jobs (imports, exports, publishing...) of all users; select Active jobs only to see the running jobs, and cancel a job if needed;
- System Logs: the server log, live;
- Messages: messages sent to the users by email or as in-app notifications.
System administrators, survey managers and survey administrators also see the Changelog in the Help menu, with all the changes and fixes of each release. They can open it from the What's new in Arena dialog too, with All changes and fixes (changelog).

In Messages, click New to write a message:
- Subject and Body: the body can be formatted with Markdown and can contain the placeholders {{userTitleAndName}} and {{userName}}; switch on Preview to see the result;
- Notification Type: Email, In App Notification or both;
- Target User Type: All Users, or only some types of users, e.g. Survey Administrators (to write only to the administrators of the surveys) or Individual Users (type their email addresses in Included Emails); use Excluded Emails to leave out some users;
- Date Valid Until: until when the in-app notification is shown.
Click Send Message. The message is sent in the background and a progress dialog is shown: you can cancel the sending from it. Messages already sent are not recalled.
